/P 104 0 R
/T (Although the Constitution does not explicitly dictate the manner in which electoral districts should be drawn, the task is mainly left to state legislatures, resulting in a complicated scheme of redistricting across the United States. The concept of gerrymandering refers to the deliberate shaping of the boundaries of electoral districts in order to benefit one political party over another. The practice developed after the foundation of the country, but today, developed technologies openly allow numerous political actors to reach their goals of winning elections through scientifically-based gerrymandering. Candidates and parties that engage in the practice of gerrymandering should consider one essential question, namely whether Congress may impose a nationwide ban of gerrymandering and how it can enforce this requirement without going against the principles of federalism. )
/E (Although the Constitution does not explicitly dictate the manner in which electoral districts should be drawn, the task is mainly left to state legislatures, resulting in a complicated scheme of redistricting across the United States. The concept of gerrymandering refers to the deliberate shaping of the boundaries of electoral districts in order to benefit one political party over another. The practice developed after the foundation of the country, but today, developed technologies openly allow numerous political actors to reach their goals of winning elections through scientifically-based gerrymandering. Candidates and parties that engage in the practice of gerrymandering should consider one essential question, namely whether Congress may impose a nationwide ban of gerrymandering and how it can enforce this requirement without going against the principles of federalism. )
/Pg 13 0 R
