/T (Opponents raise two central objections. The first is the escalation problem: if one party expands the Court while it holds power, nothing stops the next party from doing the same the next time it controls government, which could produce a spiral where the Court's size grows every time political control flips, eventually undermining the very idea of a stable, independent judiciary. The second is the 1937 precedent itself. Even a Senate supermajority of 76 Democrats to 16 Republicans was not enough to pass Roosevelt's plan once it was perceived as a raw power grab rather than a genuine reform \(Fox News\), a powerful historical signal that public trust, not vote count alone, determines whether expansion is politically survivable. )
/E (Opponents raise two central objections. The first is the escalation problem: if one party expands the Court while it holds power, nothing stops the next party from doing the same the next time it controls government, which could produce a spiral where the Court's size grows every time political control flips, eventually undermining the very idea of a stable, independent judiciary. The second is the 1937 precedent itself. Even a Senate supermajority of 76 Democrats to 16 Republicans was not enough to pass Roosevelt's plan once it was perceived as a raw power grab rather than a genuine reform \(Fox News\), a powerful historical signal that public trust, not vote count alone, determines whether expansion is politically survivable. )
