/T (Finally, the delegates need to understand that introducing the ban on partisan gerrymandering at the federal level requires a political battle in the senate, where the rules allow a filibuster to block any legislative action. Gerrymandering is used to gain representation for one party and therefore violates the principles of the separation of powers and checks and balances. A ban on it will damage the powers of one of the chambers of Congress, which will certainly oppose the bill, and the delegates will have to find a way to get the necessary 60 votes to proceed to the vote. )
