1310 0 obj
<<
/Title (This debate sits at theintersection of two competing values central to American democracy: judicial independence, the ideathat courts must be insulated from political pressure to rule fairly, and democratic accountability,the idea that no unelected body should be permanently shielded from the political process that produced it. Delegates will need to grapple with both as they negotiate the Senate's response.)
/Dest [12 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1230 0 R
/Prev 1297 0 R
/First 1325 0 R
/Last 1325 0 R
/Count -1
/Next 1298 0 R
>>
endobj
1311 0 obj
<<
/Title (Although the Constitution does not explicitly dictate the manner in which electoral districts should be drawn, the task is mainly left to state legislatures, resulting in a complicated scheme of redistricting across the United States. The concept of gerrymandering refers to the deliberate shaping of the boundaries of electoral districts in order to benefit one political party over another. The practice developed after the foundation of the country, but today, developed technologies openly allow numerous political actors to reach their goals of winning elections through scientifically-based gerrymandering. Candidates and parties that engage in the practice of gerrymandering should consider one essential question, namely whether Congress may impose a nationwide ban of gerrymandering and how it can enforce this requirement without going against the principles of federalism.)
/Dest [13 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1299 0 R
/Next 1326 0 R
>>
endobj
1312 0 obj
<<
/Title (Plenary sessions will be held by delegates to analyze possible measures regarding redistricting reform, such as requiring independent redistricting commissions, using uniform statistical methods to assess fairness in redistricting maps and reinforcing the Voting Rights Law. The study will investigate whether federal regulation of districting practices is an important safeguard against any possible violation of the right to vote or whether that would be regarded as dishonest interference in state matters.)
/Dest [14 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1299 0 R
/Prev 1327 0 R
>>
endobj
