/Title (The main issueofthecontroversy over the federal ban of gerrymandering is the balance between federal oversightandstateautonomy. The proponents of the idea appeal to Article I, Section 4 of the US Constitution,betterknownas the Elections Clause, which allows Congress to legislate to regulate federalelections.However, supporters of state autonomy argue that the process has always been under thejurisdictionofthe states as the Tenth Amendment secures the powers not explicitly granted to thefederalgovernment, making such intervention unconstitutional and unnecessary.)
/Dest [20 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1397 0 R
/Next 1421 0 R
>>
endobj
1409 0 obj
<<
/Title (Finally, the delegates need to understand that introducing the ban on partisan gerrymandering at the federal level requires a political battle in the senate, where the rules allow a filibuster to block any legislative action. Gerrymandering is used to gain representation for one party and therefore violates the principles of the separation of powers and checks and balances. A ban on it will damage the powers of one of the chambers of Congress, which will certainly oppose the bill, and the delegates will have to find a way to get the necessary 60 votes to proceed to the vote.)
/Dest [21 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1397 0 R
/Prev 1422 0 R
>>
endobj
