/Title (The process of partisangerrymandering dates back to the founding fathers. The word gerrymandering itself wasborn in 1812 after Massachusetts governor Elbridge Gerry drew a new legislative district with sucha strange shape that it reminded people of a salamander. A century later, many state legislatures refused to redraw districts entirely, resulting in large discrepancies in the number of people per district and giving an unfair advantage to rural districts over urban ones.)
/Dest [19 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1407 0 R
/Next 1434 0 R
>>
endobj
1420 0 obj
<<
/Title (Although the Court has consistently ruled against racially discriminatory district plans, it did not adopt a clear stance on partisan gerrymandering for many years. The issue was finally resolved in the Rucho v. Common Cause case in 2019, when the Supreme Court ruled that partisan gerrymandering was a non-justiciable political question. In other words, the federal court could not intervene in the matter, which left it to Congress and the state legislatures to solve the problem of partisan gerrymandering.)
/Dest [20 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1407 0 R
/Prev 1435 0 R
>>
endobj
