/Title (Opponents raise two central objections. The first is the escalation problem: if one party expands the Court while it holds power, nothing stops the next party from doing the same the next time it controls government, which could produce a spiral where the Court's size grows every time political control flips, eventually undermining the very idea of a stable, independent judiciary. The second is the 1937 precedent itself. Even a Senate supermajority of 76 Democrats to 16 Republicans was not enough to pass Roosevelt's plan once it was perceived as a raw power grab rather than a genuine reform \(Fox News\), a powerful historical signal that public trust, not vote count alone, determines whether expansion is politically survivable.)
/Dest [19 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1418 0 R
>>
endobj
1434 0 obj
<<
/Title (The unfairness of such a system was partially addressed in the 1960s through several court orders, including Baker v. Carr in 1962 and Reynolds v. Sims in 1964, which established the rules for redistricting, including the famous "one person, one vote" principle. At the same time, the Voting Rights Act of 1965 was passed, which prohibited discrimination against voters based on race, and a rule banning discriminatory partisan gerrymandering was introduced.)
/Dest [19 0 R /Fit]
