/Title (Proponents of amending the Judiciary Act generally make three related arguments. First, that the Court's legitimacy has already suffered from increasingly political confirmation battles, and that expansion is a corrective response to a Court that no longer reflects broad public consensus rather than a new escalation of hostilities. Second, that the number nine itself has no special constitutional or moral status, it is simply the most recent number Congress happened to choose in 1869, no more)
/Dest [18 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1432 0 R
/First 1454 0 R
/Last 1454 0 R
/Count -1
/Next 1446 0 R
>>
endobj
1446 0 obj
<<
/Title (sacred than the six, seven, or ten Justices the Court has had at other points in history. Third, that treating the Court's composition as permanently untouchable effectively freezes the judiciary's ideological balance for a generation or more each time a presidency and Senate align on appointments, which some argueis itself a form of undemocratic entrenchment.)
/Dest [19 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1432 0 R
/Prev 1445 0 R
>>
